r)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBog">OCLC</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBok"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/1346351849" class="external text" id="mwBoo">1346351849</a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=Pseudoscience+in+Therapy%3A+A+Skeptical+Field+Guide&amp;rft.place=Cambridge%2C+UK%3B+New+York&amp;rft.pub=Cambridge+University+Press&amp;rft.date=2023&amp;rft_id=info%3Aoclcnum%2F1346351849&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1017%2F9781009000611&amp;rft.isbn=978-1-009-00510-4&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APsychotherapy" class="Z3988" about="#mwt27" id="mwBos"></span> For example, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Thought_Field_Therapy" title="Thought Field Therapy" id="mwBow">Thought Field Therapy</a> has been called a pseudoscience: <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt29" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Lilienfeld"},"first":{"wt":"Scott O."},"author-link":{"wt":"Scott Lilienfeld"},"date":{"wt":"December 2015"},"title":{"wt":"Introduction to special section on pseudoscience in psychiatry"},"journal":{"wt":"[[The Canadian Journal of Psychiatry]]"},"volume":{"wt":"60"},"issue":{"wt":"12"},"pages":{"wt":"531–533"},"pmid":{"wt":"26720820"},"pmc":{"wt":"4679160"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1177/070674371506001202"},"quote":{"wt":"Although the boundaries separating pseudoscience from science are fuzzy, pseudosciences are characterized by several warning signs—fallible but useful indicators that distinguish them from most scientific disciplines.&amp;nbsp;... In contrast to most accepted medical interventions, which are prescribed for a circumscribed number of conditions, many pseudoscientific techniques lack boundary conditions of application. For example, some proponents of [[Thought Field Therapy]], an intervention that purports to correct imbalances in unobservable energy fields, using specified bodily tapping algorithms, maintain that it can be used to treat virtually any psychological condition, and that it is helpful not only for adults but also for children, dogs, and horses."}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBo0"/><cite id="CITEREFLilienfeld2015" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt29"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Scott_Lilienfeld" title="Scott Lilienfeld" id="mwBo4">Lilienfeld, Scott O.</a> (December 2015). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4679160" class="external text" id="mwBo8">"Introduction to special section on pseudoscience in psychiatry"</a>. <i id="mwBpA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./The_Canadian_Journal_of_Psychiatry" title="The Canadian Journal of Psychiatry" id="mwBpE">The Canadian Journal of Psychiatry</a></i>. <b id="mwBpI">60</b> (12): <span class="nowrap" id="mwBpM">531–</span>533. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBpQ">doi</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1177%2F070674371506001202" class="external text" id="mwBpU">10.1177/070674371506001202</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMC_(identifier)" title="PMC (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBpY">PMC</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBpc"> </span><span class="id-lock-free" title="Freely accessible" id="mwBpg"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4679160" class="external text" id="mwBpk">4679160</a></span>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBpo">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBps"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26720820" class="external text" id="mwBpw">26720820</a>. <q id="mwBp0">Although the boundaries separating pseudoscience from science are fuzzy, pseudosciences are characterized by several warning signs—fallible but useful indicators that distinguish them from most scientific disciplines.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBp4"> </span>... In contrast to most accepted medical interventions, which are prescribed for a circumscribed number of conditions, many pseudoscientific techniques lack boundary conditions of application. For example, some proponents of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Thought_Field_Therapy" title="Thought Field Therapy" id="mwBp8">Thought Field Therapy</a>, an intervention that purports to correct imbalances in unobservable energy fields, using specified bodily tapping algorithms, maintain that it can be used to treat virtually any psychological condition, and that it is helpful not only for adults but also for children, dogs, and horses.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=The+Canadian+Journal+of+Psychiatry&amp;rft.atitle=Introduction+to+special+section+on+pseudoscience+in+psychiatry&amp;rft.volume=60&amp;rft.issue=12&amp;rft.pages=531-533&amp;rft.date=2015-12&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2Fpmc%2Farticles%2FPMC4679160%23id-name%3DPMC&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F26720820&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1177%2F070674371506001202&amp;rft.aulast=Lilienfeld&amp;rft.aufirst=Scott+O.&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2Fpmc%2Farticles%2FPMC4679160&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APsychotherapy" class="Z3988" about="#mwt29" id="mwBqA"></span> See also: <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt31" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"p