0952695117703243</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMC_(identifier)" title="PMC (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEjE">PMC</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEjI"> </span><span class="id-lock-free" title="Freely accessible" id="mwEjM"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5484460" class="external text" id="mwEjQ">5484460</a></span>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEjU">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEjY"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28690369" class="external text" id="mwEjc">28690369</a>. <q id="mwEjg">Michel Foucault, in what has perhaps become the most well-known critique of psychiatric and therapeutic interventions, identified a shift in the way western society conceptualized madness with the establishment of 'moral treatment' at the end of the 18th century...</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=History+of+the+Human+Sciences&amp;rft.atitle=Psychotherapy+in+historical+perspective&amp;rft.volume=30&amp;rft.issue=2&amp;rft.pages=3-16&amp;rft.date=2017-04&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2Fpmc%2Farticles%2FPMC5484460%23id-name%3DPMC&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F28690369&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1177%2F0952695117703243&amp;rft.aulast=Marks&amp;rft.aufirst=Sarah&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2Fpmc%2Farticles%2FPMC5484460&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APsychotherapy" class="Z3988" id="mwEjk"></span></li><li style="margin-bottom:.5em; display:block;;margin-top:.5em;" id="mwEjo"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt718" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}' id="mwEjs"/><cite id="CITEREFGuilfoyle2005" class="citation journal cs1">Guilfoyle, Michael (February 2005). "From therapeutic power to resistance? Therapy and cultural hegemony". <i id="mwEjw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Theory_&amp;_Psychology" title="Theory &amp; Psychology" id="mwEj0">Theory &amp; Psychology</a></i>. <b id="mwEj4">15</b> (1): <span class="nowrap" id="mwEj8">101–</span>124. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEkA">doi</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1177%2F0959354305049748" class="external text" id="mwEkE">10.1177/0959354305049748</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./S2CID_(identifier)" title="S2CID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEkI">S2CID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEkM"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:145491324" class="external text" id="mwEkQ">145491324</a>. <q id="mwEkU">Foucault's views have been used to highlight problems of power in a variety of 'mental health' fields: in nursing (e.g. Clinton &amp; Hazelton, 2002), social work (e.g. Foote &amp; Frank, 1999), psychiatry (e.g. Ali, 2002), and in the cross-disciplinary practices of psychotherapy (most notably in narrative therapy—e.g. Flaskas &amp; Humphreys, 1993; Swann, 1999; White &amp; Epston, 1990). However, there is no single 'Foucauldian' approach to power, or indeed to therapy, and his ideas are used, as he intended, more in the manner of a 'tool kit' of ideas than as a coherent theoretical account.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Theory+%26+Psychology&amp;rft.atitle=From+therapeutic+power+to+resistance%3F+Therapy+and+cultural+hegemony&amp;rft.volume=15&amp;rft.issue=1&amp;rft.pages=101-124&amp;rft.date=2005-02&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1177%2F0959354305049748&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fapi.semanticscholar.org%2FCorpusID%3A145491324%23id-name%3DS2CID&amp;rft.aulast=Guilfoyle&amp;rft.aufirst=Michael&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APsychotherapy" class="Z3988" id="mwEkY"></span></li><li style="margin-bottom:.5em; display:block;" id="mwEkc"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt719" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}' id="mwEkg"/><cite id="CITEREFIsackHook1995" class="citation book cs1">Isack, Sharonne; Hook, Derek (20 October 1995). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.criticalmethods.org/hook.htm" class="external text" id="mwEkk">"The psychological imperialism of psychotherapy"</a>. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.criticalmethods.org/spanner.htm" class="external text" id="mwEko"><i id="mwEks">1st Annual South African Qualitative Methods Conference: "A spanner in the works of the factory of truth"</i></a>. Johannesburg, South Africa: Critical Methods Society. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20140419043338/http://www.criticalmethods.org/spanner.htm" class="external text" id="mwEkw">Archived</a> from the original on 19 April 2014.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=bookitem&amp;rft.atitle=The+psychological+imperialism+of+psychotherapy&amp;rft.btitle=1st+Annual+South+African+Qualitative+Methods+Conference%3A+%22A+spanner+in+the+works+of+the+factory+of+truth%22&amp;rft.place=Johannesburg%2C+South+Africa&amp;rft.pub=Critical+Methods+Society&amp;rft.date=1995-10-20&amp;rft.aulast=Isack&amp;rft.aufirst=Sharonne&amp;rft.au=Hook%2C+Derek&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.criticalmethods.org%2Fhook.htm&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APsychotherapy" class="Z3988" id="mwEk0"></span></li></ul></div></span></li>
